 \ReviewerComment{L469-471: This does not appear like an extreme precipitation. This is the reason why the modeling and analyses need to be carried out by separating seasons. According to some definitions, < 1 mm of rainfall is not even considered as rain. Could you plot the PDFs or CDFs of total precipitation across 4 seasons from your dataset (entire dataset from 2004-2021)?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We agree that Q95=0.5740~mm\,h$^{-1}$ is a relative upper-tail threshold for regional-mean hourly ERA5 precipitation, not a meteorological extreme definition. It is the untransformed 95th percentile of 18,404 training targets. The existing Methods discussion distinguishes it from WMO's intensity classification and its different temporal/spatial support. We use wet $\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with $>0$ retained only as a labelled legacy diagnostic; rainfall below 1~mm\,h$^{-1}$ is not treated as universally dry.
 
-\ChangeLocation
+Supplementary Fig.~\ref{supp-sfig:r3season_full_cdf} now shows unconditional four-season CDFs and upper-tail exceedance curves for all 26,300 retained 00/06/12/18 UTC timestamps in 2004--2021, including zeros. The earlier figure used 26,282 windowed targets, omitting six initial timestamps per partition; we corrected that scope while preserving the old figure. The seasonal Q95 values are 0.6197/0.4575/0.5877/0.6726~mm\,h$^{-1}$ in DJF/MAM/JJA/SON (Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_full_distribution}); these full-period descriptions do not change the training event threshold.
+
+We also report the existing five-seed XGBoost comparison of year-round (G), four seasonal (S) and year-round calendar-augmented (F) models, with identical meteorological inputs, splits and hyperparameters and no retuning (Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_gsf_scores} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_gsf_pairs}). Overall mean seed-level RMSE is 0.2439/0.2468/0.2443~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The S--G mean seed-level difference is +0.0029 (5/5 worse), but its block interval belongs to the distinct five-seed-mean-prediction difference +0.0020 $[-0.0012,+0.0056]$. F--G is +0.0004 $[-0.0011,+0.0018]$ on the latter scale. Seasonal S--G intervals all include zero; calendar augmentation improves JJA and worsens SON with pointwise intervals excluding zero. These unadjusted, conditional intervals do not combine training and sampling uncertainty.
 
+We therefore retain year-round models and evaluate them by season, without claiming that seasonal modelling is generally ineffective. The experiment tests only fixed-configuration XGBoost, with smaller seasonal training sets, not season-specific deep models or retuning. Existing seasonal variable-ablation and climatology results remain separate.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:seasonal_baseline_check}: full-period distributions and G/S/F sensitivity; Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}: scope and uncertainty limits. Supplementary Fig.~\ref{supp-sfig:r3season_full_cdf} and Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_full_distribution}--\ref{supp-stab:r3season_gsf_pairs}. Final page/line numbers pending full assembly.}
+
+
+\EvidenceBox{Full-period seasonal precipitation distributions}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Full-period seasonal distributions use all 26,300 retained 00/06/12/18 UTC timestamps over 2004--2021, including zero precipitation, rather than only the 26,282 windowed prediction targets (Supplementary Fig.~\ref{supp-sfig:r3season_full_cdf} and Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_full_distribution}). The 18 additional timestamps are the first six in each chronological partition, excluded only when constructing model windows. DJF/MAM/JJA/SON have 6,500/6,624/6,624/6,552 samples, with descriptive seasonal Q95 values of 0.6197/0.4575/0.5877/0.6726~mm\,h$^{-1}$; these do not redefine the fixed training threshold. Values $\geq1.0$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ account for 2.49/1.24/2.14/2.98\%, respectively. The curves describe regional-mean preceding-hour precipitation, not six-hour totals or wet-only distributions; neither Q95 nor 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ is adopted as a physical extreme-rainfall definition.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Seasonal-training sensitivity}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The separate q03d sensitivity compares year-round XGBoost (G), four season-specific models (S), and a year-round model with month/hour sine--cosine inputs (F), using five matched seeds and unchanged hyperparameters without retuning (Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_gsf_scores} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3season_gsf_pairs}). Mean seed-level overall RMSE is 0.2439/0.2468/0.2443~mm\,h$^{-1}$ for G/S/F. S is worse overall in all five seeds, but its five-seed-mean-prediction difference is +0.0020 with a pointwise 95\% circular-block interval of $[-0.0012,+0.0056]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The corresponding F difference is +0.0004 $[-0.0011,+0.0018]$; its JJA improvement and SON deterioration have intervals excluding zero, without multiplicity adjustment. None of the seasonal S--G intervals excludes zero. Thus this fixed-configuration XGBoost experiment does not show consistent benefits from seasonal specialization; we retain year-round models with seasonal evaluation, rather than infer that season-specific modelling is ineffective.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Scope and uncertainty limits}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The q03d seasonal-training sensitivity did not retune each season or train season-specific deep models. Seasonal training uses roughly one quarter of the available training targets, and the experiment does not isolate the reason for its score changes. Five seeds describe training variability, whereas the paired circular-block intervals condition on their mean predictions; these are separate uncertainty summaries. Full-period seasonal distribution plots are descriptive and do not enter model fitting or threshold selection.}
+}
