 \subsection*{Comment: Lines~752--758}
 
 \ReviewerComment{L752-758: Did you try a new model by reducing the historical window from the current 30 h to say 12 h to see if there is any difference in skill?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this suggestion. Yes: we compared a 12-h history (three six-hourly snapshots, $t_0-12$ to $t_0$) with the original 30-h history (six snapshots) for all six models, using five matched seeds, unchanged within-model hyperparameters and training protocol, and 3,939 common test targets (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results}; Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models}). All six models had lower mean test RMSE with 12~h. BiLSTM-Attention changed from 0.2429 to 0.2410~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (paired difference $-0.0018$~mm\,h$^{-1}$; all five seeds improved and both interval types excluded zero), and Transformer from 0.2600 to 0.2468~mm\,h$^{-1}$, although the Transformer retained the 30-epoch cap, so its gain cannot be attributed to window length alone. XGBoost changed little (0.2439 to 0.2430~mm\,h$^{-1}$; both intervals included zero). For BU-LSTM, BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention, three or four of five seeds improved and the seed-based intervals included zero. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost remained first and second, whereas BU-LSTM and BU-LSTM-Attention exchanged positions. A wider comparison of 6--66~h for XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}) gave their lowest test RMSE at 12~h and 6~h, respectively; 30~h was therefore not the best window in these comparisons. We retain 30~h in the main results because it is the original specification underlying the other analyses, and selecting a window from these test-period comparisons would use test information. These sensitivity checks are retrospective, use one chronological split and were not repeated across rolling-origin folds (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models}).}
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\ManuscriptChange{No new manuscript or Supplementary change was made for this comment. The window sensitivity results added in response to Lines~329--333 are in Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models} (Forecasting Models) and~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results} (Overall Forecasting Performance and Robustness) and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}; the two excerpts below reproduce that existing text verbatim. Final page and line numbers pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Window sensitivity results (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Input-history sensitivity does not establish 30 h as optimal. On 3,939 common test targets, changing from 30 h to 12 h lowers mean RMSE for all six tested models; BiLSTM-Attention changes from 0.2429 to 0.2410~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and Transformer from 0.2600 to 0.2468~mm\,h$^{-1}$. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost remain first and second, while BU-LSTM and BU-LSTM-Attention exchange positions; these are q14 reruns, not replacements for the original main-table runs. The wider two-model comparison uses 3,933 common targets: BiLSTM-Attention has its lowest numerical test RMSE with 6 h (0.2406 versus 0.2430 for 30 h), whereas XGBoost is lowest with 12 h. BiLSTM-Attention at 42/66 h improves numerically over 30 h, but both all-target interval types include zero; longer histories therefore do not show a consistent all-target advantage. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve} report seed variability and the separate block-sampling diagnostic without multiplicity adjustment. The Transformer comparison retains the 30-epoch cap and cannot isolate window length from optimization effects. Temporal SHAP concentrated toward recent snapshots is a conditional model diagnostic, not evidence for storm lifetime or a physical advection timescale.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Window specification and comparison design (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The six-snapshot (30 h) history is the original modelling specification, rather than a window justified by measured storm-passage durations. We found no independently verifiable record linking its initial choice to such durations, a specific literature recommendation or a systematic window search; its pre-experimental selection history is therefore not established. Retrospective sensitivity checks compare 12 h and 30 h for six models, and 6, 12, 18, 30, 42 and 66 h for XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention, using five matched seeds and common test times within each comparison (Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}). Shorter windows yield more training/validation samples, so these are sensitivity checks of the full window specification, not comparisons with identical fitted sample sets.}
+}
 
