 \ReviewerComment{L822-824: Is the difference statistically significant? Is it worth using a slightly sophisticated model for a marginal gain in skill?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\makeatletter
+\expandafter\let\csname r@sec:rolling_origin_results\expandafter\endcsname\csname r@manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_results\endcsname
+\makeatother
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for these questions. The difference in question is the attention gain in the one-layer BiLSTM (0.2447 to 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$). Using five matched seeds and paired comparisons on the 3,939 common test targets, the reduction was $-0.0018$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($-0.73\%$); all five seeds improved, and both the seed-t and moving-block intervals excluded zero. After Holm adjustment over the 16 all-target RMSE comparisons, however, the adjusted $p$ values were 0.17 and 0.13, and the wet and upper-tail differences were not clearly separated. We therefore no longer describe attention as a demonstrated benefit. On the second question, the added complexity is not justified by point accuracy alone in this setting. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost differed by only $-0.43\%$ overall, with both intervals including zero; XGBoost was better in the wet and upper-tail subsets; the three probabilistic recurrent models had 1.3--2.2\% higher overall RMSE than XGBoost; and adding attention to BU-LSTM gave no clear RMSE or CRPS improvement despite 159,272 additional parameters. The neural models also required about 105--393~s of recorded training per seed, compared with 0.1--0.4~s for XGBoost, although these timings are not a controlled benchmark and inference cost was not measured. For point forecasts in this setting, XGBoost therefore provided comparable overall accuracy with much shorter recorded training; because these run times are not a unified computational-cost benchmark, we do not claim a general efficiency ranking. The probabilistic recurrent models are useful only where predictive distributions are required, subject to their upper-tail undercoverage. These conclusions are restricted to this regional ERA5 target, lead time and set of configurations. The revised discussion is in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_discussion}, and the paired comparisons and recorded costs are in Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_pairs} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_cost}.}
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_discussion} (Overall Forecasting Performance and Model Robustness): the original attention paragraph is revised with paired and Holm-adjusted results, and a new paragraph assesses complexity against accuracy. Supplementary Material: new final section ``Paired model comparisons and complexity'' with Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_pairs} (16 model pairs, all/wet/upper-tail RMSE and two CRPS comparisons) and~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_cost} (parameters and recorded training times with their limitations). Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:seed_stability} and the existing figures are unchanged; no training or data download. Final page and line numbers pending full-manuscript assembly.}
 
+\EvidenceBox{Attention gain and multiplicity adjustment (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_discussion})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The comparison between BiLSTM-Attention and the otherwise identical BiLSTM-NoAttention control isolates the temporal attention layer. Attention reduced mean RMSE from 0.2447 to 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (paired difference $-0.0018$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, $-0.73\%$); all five seeds improved, and both the seed-t and moving-block intervals, each $[-0.0033,-0.0003]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, excluded zero. After Holm adjustment over the 16 all-target RMSE comparisons in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_pairs}, however, the adjusted $p$ values were 0.17 (seed-t) and 0.13 (block), and the wet and upper-tail differences were not clearly separated. The attention layer added 8,320 trainable parameters (36,993 to 45,313). The gain is therefore consistent in sign but small, and it is not established after multiplicity adjustment.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Complexity versus accuracy (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_discussion})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Greater model complexity was not matched by clear gains in point accuracy. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost differed by $-0.0011$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($-0.43\%$) overall, with both intervals including zero, whereas XGBoost had lower RMSE in the wet ($+2.4\%$) and upper-tail ($+3.6\%$) subsets, with all five seeds and both intervals favouring it; in the rolling-origin evaluation, the pooled difference slightly favoured XGBoost but depended on block length (Section~\ref{sec:rolling_origin_results}). BU-LSTM, BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention had 1.3--2.2\% higher overall RMSE than XGBoost in all five seeds, and adding attention to BU-LSTM (159,272 additional parameters) produced no clear RMSE or CRPS improvement. All models except the Transformer had lower RMSE than AR(1) after Holm adjustment. In the recorded runs, training the neural models took about 105--393~s per seed, compared with 0.1--0.4~s for XGBoost, but timer definitions differ, the XGBoost device was not recorded and inference time was not benchmarked (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_cost}). For point forecasts in this setting, XGBoost therefore provided comparable overall accuracy and better wet and upper-tail accuracy, with much shorter recorded training times that do not constitute a unified computational-cost benchmark. The probabilistic models are justified only where predictive distributions are required, and their upper-tail undercoverage remains a limitation. These conclusions apply to this regional ERA5 target, the 6-h lead time and the tested configurations, without a formal cost--benefit criterion.}
+}
